—「」



# Miscellaneous Particles
ぞ is a final particle that adds force to the sentence. Kind of like an exclamation.



# Copula
でございます is the respectful-yet-humble form of です.



# Pronouns
～たち makes the pronoun plural. Except for 彼, which becomes 彼ら.



# Adjective-like
の and である may generate an attribute of unessential information. Either of these particles may be replaced with a comma. 「ゼレンスキー大統領は**ロシア軍から奪還した黒海の拠点の島**、ズミイヌイ島を訪れた。」 — "President Zelensky visited Zmiinyi Island, **an island base in the Black Sea which \[they\] recaptured from the Russian Army**."

<!-- is the continuative form essentially nominalization? -->
When needing to create an attribute with a verb at the end of a clause, turn it into its continuative form then follow it with の. 「**土砂降り**の雨」 — "**Pouring** rain." Though, using the attributive form of the verb is making a comeback.

いい is a contraction of 良い. In addition to meaning "good," it may also mean "sufficient." 

仲良し is a noun/no-adjective, but its adverbial form is 仲良く—not 仲良しに. That is not to say it is an adjective; it is derived from 仲が良い, a valid adjective.

多い and 少ない may only be in the predicate. They necessitate context and often modify 量. 少ない can still be used attributively but only with uncountable nouns with the added nuance of insufficiency. 

同じに is an adverb meaning "the same." 同じく differs by relating two things to be *simply* the same. 同じように means "similar." With the なら particle, 同じ becomes "anyhow." 

～風 is a suffix that attaches to nouns to create "something like {{ noun }}." Combined creates an adjectival noun that uses の. 



# Verbs
かつて with the negative can be interpreted as "never before."

～てくる is the grammar for coming back after whatever action it's attached to.

心地 may follow the stem of verbs (e.g., 生きる -> 生き) to create "the sense of doing {{ action }}."

～仕込み attaches to a place to mean "acquired at {{ place }}." 

だ might go between the quote and と if there is strong affirmation that whatever entity it commentates on fits the description described by the quote.

に can mark the agent in potential and existential sentences. Adjectival predicates that refer to quantity also fall into this category.



# ある & いる
ある can be used to discuss situations that the agent finds themselves in. For example, 「**お客様の担当ドライバーが薬物またはアルコールの影響下**にあるとお考えの場合は、すぐに運転を中止するよう要求してください。」 — "In the event that **you believe your designated driver is under the influence of drugs and/or alcohol**, please immediately request to cancel your ride."

<!-- と is the quotation particle -->
とある is a generic for the situation being quoted? Often written as と書いてある. 「聖書には「初に神は天と地とを創造された」とある。」 — "It is written in the Bible that, 'In the beginning, God created the heavens and the earth.'"



# Questions
If the question is actually negative, responding with はい means agreeing with the negative status. Essentially, はい translates to "right." 

<!-- TODO: idk -->
のじゃない and stuff seem to not take a preceding noun well. Must be separated with な?



# も
XもX is used to say how something is so much the case.



# Numbers
零 has some flexibility around 0. 

4 may be pronounced シ or よん, with the latter being more common. 7 is シチ or なな. 9 is キュウ or ク, with the former being common. All other numbers typically use their Sino-Japanese pronunciation. 

When counting up or down, the Sino-Japanese pronunciations win all around. Additionally, all single mora pronunciations are extended to two (e.g., 2 becomes ニー). Finally, the intonation goes high to low for each number. The elongation also applies to phone numbers, as well as zero being pronounced レイ and dashes being written as の.
- 110: Police, ヒャクとおバン
- 119: Ambulance / fire brigade, ヒャクジュウキュウバン

After 20, intonation becomes per digit. The other significant change is that the ones place is high to low. If the digit is zero, it may appear as 〇 (read as とんで) in certain circumstances, as does two being pronounced ふた. 

Like how each English counting unit has a ones, tens, and hundreds, Japanese units goes to the thousands. Meaning there's no 万億—it's 兆.

Arabic numerals are used up until 1000. However, sometimes each digit is substituted with kanji (e.g., 一一 is 11).

Native pronunciations only persist for 1-10. Additionally, they might be reduced down to just the first mora. The つ counter doesn't attach to 10. 
- 1: ひと
- 2: ふた
- 3: み
- 4: よ
- 5: いつ
- 6: む
- 7: なな
- 8: や
- 9: ここの
- 10: とお

Counters are either adverbs or adverbial nouns—can either be an adverb or noun.

何～ serves as a "how many (of this counter)." However, if no kanji counter is appropriate, use いくつ.

For certain numbers, the counter's pronunciation typically follows a pattern in how it changes.
- 1: /k/, /s/, /t/, and /h/ turn イチ to イッ
- 3: /s/ becomes /z/, /sh/ to /j/, /k/ to /g/, and /h/ to /b/ or /p/
- 4: /h/ turns into /b/ or /p/
- 8: /k/, /s/, /t/, or /h/ make ハチ to ハッ
- 10: /k/, /s/, /t/, or /h/ turn ジュウ to ジュッ or ジッ
- 100: /k/ or /h/ transform ヒャク to ヒャッ

～度 can either count degree or occurrences with uncertain or irregular recurrences. ～回 is more suited for cyclical behavior and is often used in speech unconditionally. Stacking numbers (e.g., 四五) before ～度 means "number or number." Similarly, ～回 means "number, number." 数 as the "number" can mean "several." 数 attaches to the end of the counters *without numbers* to turn "number of times" into a noun. Confusingly, 何 can be used as a number to mean "no matter how many times," though, with ～回, a number is inserted in between because it's the exact same scenario repeating itself. 幾度も and 幾度となく do mean "many times." もう一度 means "one more time," but it places heavy emphasis on the reoccurrence, unlike もう一回.

A count may be followed by ～かそこいら to create "approximately." It has the added nuance of not being over.



# Time
There are some alternate deictic time phrases that are also common and/or have a special connotation.
- こんにち (今日) means "these days" or "nowadays"
- 今日の夕方 refers to the time before it gets completely dark
- あす (明日) is also common and may also mean "near future"
- 明日の～ is most common for tomorrow morning, evening, etc.
- あさっての～ (明後日の～) is for morning after tomorrow, etc.
- しあさって (明々後日) and やのあさって (明々々後日) are for three and four days in the future respectively
- 昨日の～ is for last whatever
- さくや (昨夜) is common
- 夕べ is common in literature, and it may even be written as 昨夜.
- いっさく (一昨) is for morning before yesterday, etc.
- さきおととい (一昨昨日) and さきおととし (一昨々年) for three days/years before

Years sometimes have a prefix to indicate the year system being used.

０時 and ２４時 both refer to midnight. ０時 is preferred, and both are often pronounced as just the number in casual speech (i.e., ゼロ and ニジュウよん).

Words like 朝 can serve as adverbs to a time of day (e.g., ７時) to be abundantly clear. They may also indicate the 12-hour system much like 午後 does. ３０分 can be replaced with 半. Likewise, "a little past" and "a little before" can be achieved with 過ぎ and 前 respectively. The "a little" is actually a lie; the range could be instant to a fair amount.

The 翌 and 当 prefixes' phrases are not followed by に by nature. They mean "next" and "the (xxx) in question" respectively. 翌 may also attach to dates. 

明くる is like 翌, but it only attaches to non-deictic time phrases. 翌々 doubles it—it means following x2.

The ～越し suffix indicates that something was so during the time period the suffix is attached to. Similarly, ～前 attaches "a little before." ～過ぎ is "a little after." 

Although synonyms, 時間 leans more towards hours, while 時刻 refers to the period of time.

For 時, だった in the dependent clause can be replaced with の if there are little feelings held toward the clause. Unless the subject is different in the main clause.



# Day-to-day
When talking about one's own job, をしている and the copula are mostly interchangeable. However, the former is better for describing a job for putting food on the table. The latter is better when the job is more so part of their identity or the job has modifiers put on it.

医者 is a more colloquial way of saying "doctor" than 医師.

前 as a prefix to job titles refers to the immediate previous position holder, unlike 元～, which may refer to any of them.

There are two words for continent: 大陸 and 大州. The latter is closer to the geological definition. 

Every country has a kanji spelling, but loanwords are often preferred. Additionally, these kanji spellings tend to get abbreviated to one kanji. These abbreviations may be combined, but if the combination is uncommon, a ・ is inserted.

Saying "no" tends to be too direct, so Japanese speakers tend to say it in a roundabout way. 

Although いえ and いや have the same nuances as いいえ, they do have some extra like criticizing the presentation of information. いや is the only one that does that while not canceling the information itself. いや may also be used when talking to oneself.